    "recommendations": "Reducing the continuity of shrub cover surrounding the structure and creating separation between brush and the building perimeter would improve defensible space and reduce opportunities for fire to reach the structure. Where shrubs are adjacent to the structure, clearing or thinning vegetation to establish a buffer zone around the foundation, walls, and any attached features may reduce direct fuel contact and support suppression operations. Managing heavy ground vegetation within the immediate structure zone could further reduce fuel continuity across the property. Maintaining the access route by reducing vegetation encroachment along its edges would support firefighter access and improve conditions for emergency response and evacuation. Given the isolated nature of the structure within open terrain at the wildland interface, sustaining a well-managed perimeter of reduced fuels around the structure and keeping the access route clear of overhanging or encroaching vegetation would contribute meaningfully to long-term wildfire resilience and property preparedness."
